      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The 50% DA Trigger: How the Ceiling Reached ₹25 Lakh</h2>
        <p>
          A vital provision introduced by the 7th Central Pay Commission was an automatic inflation indexation mechanism for retirement gratuity:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The 25% Automatic Escalation Clause:</h4>
          <p>
            The 7th CPC explicitly recommended that <em>"the maximum limit of retirement gratuity and death gratuity shall be enhanced by 25% whenever the Dearness Allowance rises by 50% of the basic pay."</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            When the Central Government officially notified that the Dearness Allowance payable to Central Government employees crossed the 50% threshold effective January 1, 2024, the Department of Pension and Pensioners' Welfare (DoPPW) issued an Office Memorandum officially raising the maximum limit from <strong>₹20 Lakh to ₹25 Lakh</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Crucially, this ₹25 Lakh limit applies strictly to Central Government civil servants covered under the CCS (Pension) Rules or the CCS (Payment of Gratuity under National Pension System) Rules, 2021, and serves as the baseline launching pad for the 8th Pay Commission.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Death Gratuity Slabs: Enhanced Safety for Bereaved Families</h2>
        <p>
          While retirement gratuity requires a minimum qualifying service of <strong>5 years</strong>, <strong>Death Gratuity</strong> is payable to the nominated family members of a government servant who dies while in active service, regardless of whether they completed five years.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under Rule 45(1)(b) of the CCS (Pension) Rules, death gratuity is disbursed according to a progressive tiered formula based on service tenure:
        </p>

        <div class="table-scroll-container">
          <table class="editorial-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Length of Qualifying Service</th>
                <th>Admissible Rate of Death Gratuity</th>
                <th>Illustrative Payout (Emoluments ₹60,000)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Less than 1 Year</strong></td>
                <td>2 times monthly emoluments</td>
                <td>₹1,20,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>1 Year or more but less than 5 Years</strong></td>
                <td>6 times monthly emoluments</td>
                <td>₹3,60,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>5 Years or more but less than 11 Years</strong></td>
                <td>12 times monthly emoluments</td>
                <td>₹7,20,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>11 Years or more but less than 20 Years</strong></td>
                <td>20 times monthly emoluments</td>
                <td>₹12,00,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>20 Years or more</strong></td>
                <td>Half of emoluments for every completed SMP, subject to maximum 33 times emoluments (capped at statutory ceiling)</td>
                <td>Up to ₹19,80,000 (or statutory cap)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Income Tax Exemptions Under Section 10(10)</h2>
        <p>
          One of the greatest financial protections afforded to government retirement benefits is its statutory tax status under the <strong>Indian Income Tax Act, 1961</strong>:
        </p>
        <div class="tax-guidance-card">
          <h4>Section 10(10)(i) Full Tax Immunity:</h4>
          <p>
            Under <strong>Section 10(10)(i)</strong> of the Income Tax Act, any death-cum-retirement gratuity received by Central Government civil servants, defence forces, state government personnel, and municipal employees is <strong>100% exempt from income tax</strong> up to the full statutory ceiling sanctioned by the government.
          </p>
          <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
            <li>For Central Government personnel, the full disbursed amount up to the statutory ceiling (currently ₹25 Lakh, projected ₹35L–₹50L under 8th CPC) is completely excluded from gross taxable income.</li>
            <li>Unlike private-sector gratuity which is governed under Section 10(10)(ii) and 10(10)(iii) (where notifications often trail central revisions), government employees enjoy immediate statutory tax protection on gazetted ceiling expansions.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

